 "title": "Min Stack",
 "slug": "min-stack",
 "category": "stack",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/min-stack/",
 "pattern": "Second stack that tracks the minimum",
 "statement": "Design a stack that supports `push`, `pop`, `top`, and `getMin` — where `getMin` returns the smallest value currently in the stack — with **every operation in O(1) time**.",
 "example": {
  "input": "MinStack()\npush(5)\npush(3)\npush(7)\ngetMin()  →  3\npop()\npop()\ngetMin()  →  5\ntop()  →  5",
  "output": "",
  "why": "After popping 7 and 3 only 5 is left, so it is both the top and the minimum."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "MinStack()\npush(-2)\npush(0)\npush(-3)\ngetMin()  →  -3\npop()\ntop()  →  0\ngetMin()  →  -2",
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "A normal stack gives push, pop and top in O(1). The hard part is `getMin` — scanning the stack would be O(n).",
  "Trick: keep a **second stack** whose top is always the current minimum.",
  "Push onto it only when the new value is ≤ the current minimum. Pop from it only when the value leaving the main stack *is* that minimum. Then the old minimum is right underneath, ready to take over."
 ],
 "code": "class MinStack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stack = []\n        self.min_stack = []\n\n    def push(self, val: int) -> None:\n        self.stack.append(val)\n        if not self.min_stack or val <= self.min_stack[-1]:\n            self.min_stack.append(val)\n\n    def pop(self) -> None:\n        if self.stack:\n            if self.stack[-1] == self.min_stack[-1]:\n                self.min_stack.pop()\n            self.stack.pop()\n\n    def top(self) -> int:\n        if self.stack:\n            return self.stack[-1]\n\n    def getMin(self) -> int:\n        if self.min_stack:\n            return self.min_stack[-1]",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(1)",
  "timeWhy": "Every operation only touches the tops of the two stacks.",
  "space": "O(n)",
  "spaceWhy": "In the worst case (values pushed in decreasing order) both stacks hold n values."
 },
